 "texts": [
  {
   "title": "Credit",
   "text": "Based on Aion FX's Refractor (document 2.0.1): its circuit, values and build ideas.\nThe drawing, notes and wording are Solum Builder's own.\nhttps://aionfx.com/project/refractor-professional-overdrive/",
   "pos": [
    -60,
    760
   ]
  }
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "Try: germanium for D1, D2",
   "text": "D1, D2: the original's germaniums read about 0.35 V at 5 mA on a Peak Atlas DCA55. The drawing has Aion's BAT41 Schottkys; Russian D9E (or D9B, D9D, D9J, D9K, D9L) come close, while other 1N34As sound different. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "Try: C14 for more treble",
   "text": "C14 at 6n8 or 8n2 instead of 3n9 lifts the treble; a popular change. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "Try: C1 for bass guitar",
   "text": "C1 390n film suits a bass guitar. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "About C16",
   "text": "C16 is a 1µ tantalum in the original and Aion's board (+ to R7); drawn as film, our usual signal-path advice. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "Try: other op-amps",
   "text": "Op-amps to try: LF353 or JRC4558D for IC1, NE5532 for IC2 (TL072s drawn). (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "About C9, C10",
   "text": "C9, C10: drawn as 1µ film; the original's electrolytics go + to IC1A (C9) and + to R16 (C10), the DC sides. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "The classic setting",
   "text": "The classic setting: Gain low (9-10 o'clock), Output high, into a clean valve amp. (from Aion FX's Refractor notes)"
  },
  {
   "title": "Bypass board",
   "text": "Aion's true bypass / buffer slide switch, as the KTR's. SW1, a 3PDT on-on slide or toggle, sets Bypass mode: Buffered (the original Centaur's): the input buffer always runs; bypassed, it drives the output through C2, R4. True bypass: in goes straight to out. SW2, the 3PDT footswitch (Aion's FSA, FSC; FSB lights the LED through LEDR1). Here lug 1 is On, so Aion's A1/A3 and C1/C3 trade names. R26, R27 and R28 hold whichever output isn't in use at ground, so switching doesn't pop. Bypassed in Buffered, R27 lets a little of the effect through (the original's R243 does the same). Flip both in Knobs."
  }
 ],
 "probes": {
  "in": "in",
  "out": "out"
 },
